Q1) avatar package, class+responsibilities:
*Avatar: main player entity, responsible for initializing the animations and current energy level,
 acts as publisher in the Observer pattern, updating subscribers  when energy changes.
*State:  interface for the character's behavior. has methods to handle input driven state changes
 and to execute logic rules (stateRules).
*IdleState: Implements the `State` interface. controls avatar when it rests on ground.
*RunState: Implements the `State` interface. controls avatar when running left or right on  ground.
*JumpState: Implements the `State` interface. controls avatar when its in air.
*EnergyObserver: interface for any entity that needs to know when the player's energy level moves.
*EnergyNum: text GameObject that implements EnergyObserver. receives updates the player entity

OOP Design Choices for the avatar package:
*The State Design Pattern:
 helps to separate the player's movement states into classes.
 preserves Single Responsibility Principle, also the Open/Closed Principle (easy to add new states)
*The Observer Design Pattern:
 handle updates to the user interface text. Avatar as the publisher and EnergyNum as the subscriber.
*Program to an Interface, Not an Implementation:
 avatar class interacts exclusively with the `State` and `EnergyObserver` interfaces.

Q2) trees package, class+responsibilities:
*Flora: responsible for generating trees in a given world range. Uses deterministic randomness
 so the same world area always generates the same trees.
*Tree: acts as a facade for the tree structure. Responsible for creating and managing all
 parts of a tree: trunks, leaves and fruits.
*Trunk: creates the trunk blocks of the tree. These blocks are collidable.
*Leaf: creates leaf objects and their wind animations. Responsible for rotating the leaves
 and changing their width over time.
*Fruit: creates collectible fruits. Gives energy to the avatar when collected and reappears
 after one day cycle.

OOP Design Choices for the trees package:
*The Facade Design Pattern:
 Tree acts as a facade for the tree structure. The game manager does not need to know how
 trunks, leaves and fruits are created. It only interacts with the Tree object.
*The Factory Method Pattern:
 Tree, Trunk, Leaf and Fruit use static create methods to encapsulate object creation and
 initialization logic.
*Single Responsibility Principle:
 Each class is responsible for one part of the tree system. Flora generates trees, Tree manages
 tree components, Trunk creates trunks, Leaf handles animations and Fruit handles energy
 collection and respawning.
*Deterministic Tree Generation:
 Flora generates trees using a deterministic random seed based on the world seed and tree
 position. This guarantees that revisiting the same location recreates the same trees.
*Program to an Interface, Not an Implementation:
 Fruit does not depend directly on the Avatar class.
 Instead, a Consumer<Float> callback is passed through Flora and Tree into Fruit.
 When collected, the fruit invokes the callback to update the player's energy.
 This keeps the vegetation package independent from the avatar implementation.

Q3) More classes we added are:
MiniWorld: Represents a world of the game in a fixed X range.
Its job is to tracks all the GameObject components such as blocks and trees in its x range.
OOP Design Choices :
Encapsulation+Information Hiding: The cleanup is hidden inside MiniWorld.remove().
Single Responsibility Principle: MiniWorld handles local world cycle management for X range.

Q4) changes in API:
We added a public constant (GROUND_TAG) to Block for avoiding magic strings.
No existing API methods were modified.